<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Durbe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13 July <a href="/years/1260">1260</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · <a href="/today-in-history/july-13">On this day: 13 July</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-crusade">Livonian Crusade</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Durbe was fought on 13 July 1260 near Durbe in present-day western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a>, where Samogitian forces routed the combined army of the Livonian Order and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>. About 150 knights died, including the Livonian master Burchard von Hornhausen. The defeat set off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-prussian-uprising">Great Prussian Uprising</a> and revolts among the Curonians and other Baltic peoples, and it led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindaugas">Mindaugas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> to break with the order.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-durbe.jpg" alt="Battle of Durbe" width="320" height="278" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: S. Bollmann · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Teutonic_Order_1260.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Livonian Order had been fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samogitians">Samogitians</a> since 1253, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindaugas">Mindaugas</a> was crowned King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and transferred parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samogitia">Samogitia</a> to the order. The Samogitians did not recognise the transfer. For the knights the region was strategically important, since it separated their Prussian and Livonian branches. After the Samogitians killed 12 knights at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaipeda">Memel</a> in 1257, a two-year truce followed. Once it expired they invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a> and defeated the knights at the Battle of Skuodas in 1259, which encouraged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semigallians">Semigallians</a> to rebel. The knights then built fortresses at Dobele and at Georgenburg in Samogitia, and the Samogitians cut Georgenburg off from its supplies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Livonian master Burkhard von Hornhausen raised a large army and on 25 January 1260 obtained a papal bull from Pope Alexander IV blessing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>. The Prussian and Livonian forces and their allies gathered at Memel Castle, meaning to reinforce the besieged Georgenburg. Learning that a large Samogitian force was raiding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a>, they marched into present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a> and met the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samogitians">Samogitians</a> on the southern shore of Lake Durbe on 13 July 1260.</p><p>The knights were divided. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> refused to dismount from heavy horses that were ill suited to the swampy ground. When the fighting began, the local Curonians abandoned the knights because the knights refused to release Curonian captives from the Samogitian camp, and Peter von Dusburg even alleged that the Curonians attacked from the rear. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a> and other local people fled soon afterwards. The knights were surrounded, and some 150 of them died, among them Hornhausen and the Prussian land marshal Heinrich Botel, along with hundreds of secular knights and soldiers. No contemporary source names the Samogitian leader: a chronicle of about 1517 to 1526 says <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treniota">Treniota</a>, while Inga Baranauskiene argued that it was Alminas, an elder.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>It was by far the largest defeat of the knights in the 13th century; in the second largest, at Aizkraukle, 71 knights were killed. It inspired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-prussian-uprising">Great Prussian Uprising</a>, which lasted from 1260 to 1274, and revolts by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semigallians">Semigallians</a>, Curonians and Oeselians. The battle undid some 20 years of Livonian conquest, and it took about thirty years for the order to restore its control. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treniota">Treniota</a> is alleged to have persuaded his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindaugas">Mindaugas</a> to renounce <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and break the peace with the order, and in 1263 Treniota killed Mindaugas and took the throne.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Durbe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Great Prussian Uprising, which followed the defeat of the Order's Livonian branch at the <b>Battle of Durbe</b> in 1260, nearly drove the knights out of Prussia before reinforcements restored control.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-the-teutonic-order">State of the Teutonic Order · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Livonian Order and the Bishopric of Courland conquered them between 1240 and 1267, despite a great uprising after the Order's defeat at <b>Durbe</b> in 1260.</blockquote><a href="/state/curonians">Curonians</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Samogitians crushed the Livonian Order at <b>Durbe</b> in 1260, Mindaugas broke with the order and seems to have abandoned Christianity.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-lithuania">Kingdom of Lithuania</a></li><li><blockquote>Defeats included Lake Peipus in 1242 against Alexander Nevsky and <b>Durbe</b> in 1260, but it conquered Courland and Semigallia and bought Danish Estonia in 1346.</blockquote><a href="/state/livonian-order">Livonian Order</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Order's defeat at <b>Durbe</b> in 1260, Herkus Monte led the Great Prussian Uprising, winning at Pokarwis in 1261.</blockquote><a href="/state/natangians">Natangians</a></li></ul></section>
